# state/*.jsonl are append-only logs — every channel writes to the same
# handful of files (rejected.jsonl, feedback.jsonl, unresolved_models.jsonl),
# and by default git cannot tell "two sides both appended a new line" from
# "two sides both edited the same line": its 3-way merge sees both diffs as
# touching the region right after the last common line and conflicts,
# even though the actual intent on both sides is just "add a line at the
# end" (reproduced and confirmed locally — a plain rebase/merge conflicts on
# this exact case). `merge=union` is git's built-in driver for this: for a
# genuinely append-only file it keeps both sides' new lines instead of
# conflicting. It is a second, more targeted layer under the
# concurrency: group already serializing the five channel workflows — that
# stops simultaneous writes from happening at all; this stops the narrow
# race window around a job's own checkout timing from still conflicting on
# a file where a conflict was never actually warranted.
state/*.jsonl merge=union
